            "text": "Breaks a signal into the sine waves that make it up: the Fourier transform. For each frequency from 0 (the steady average) up to half the sampling rate, it says how much of that wave the signal contains (the magnitude) and where in its cycle it starts (the phase).\n\nIt turns a window of samples - sound, a joint's motion over the last two seconds, a row of pixels - into a spectrum: which rhythms, vibrations or textures are in it and how strong each is. A shake shows as a peak at the shake's frequency; a slow sway as energy near 0; noise as energy spread across all of them.\n\nrfft is the version for real signals (all ordinary sensor data). A window of N samples gives N/2 + 1 frequencies.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.fft.rfft <sample rate: float>\nDefault: sample rate 1.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.fft.rfft 60\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe signal: a list or array of real values. Every input is transformed and sent.\n\nsample rate (float box, also an inlet):\nSamples per second. Only the frequencies outlet uses it: with 60 for a 60 frames per second stream, the frequencies come out in cycles per second (Hz).\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nFor a 2-D array, which way the signal runs: -1 (the default) along each row, 0 down each column - one spectrum per joint for a buffer of frames x joints.\n\nnorm:\nAn option, under the node: how the result is scaled. backward (the default) leaves the forward transform unscaled; ortho scales both ways equally; forward divides by N.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nspectrum:\nThe complex result, for np.fft.irfft or further calculation.\n\nmagnitude:\nHow much of each frequency there is.\n\nphase:\nWhere each wave starts in its cycle, in radians (-pi to pi).\n\nfrequencies:\nThe frequency of each entry, from 0 to half the sample rate. Sent first.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nnp.arange 64, times 45 degrees, through sin: a sine wave that goes round 8 times in 64 samples. Its spectrum is a single spike at entry 8, and nothing anywhere else. Change 45 to 90 and the spike moves to 16; to 22.5, and it moves to 4. A frequency that does not fit a whole number of times in the window (try 40) smears across its neighbours.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.fft.irfft turns a spectrum back into a signal. t.rfft is the PyTorch version, and spectrum analyses a stream as it arrives. The t.window nodes taper a window's ends, which reduces the smearing."
        },
